 impact on habit change.\nL48:   * Validated by governments in Germany and Denmark: trusted for digital wellbeing at scale.\nL49:   * Recommended by psychologists, therapists, and psychiatrists around the world.\nL50: cite2†Learn more → L51: > “This free app saves me 21 hours every month.”\nL52: \nL53: cite16†Watch video  ↗†www.youtube.com L54: \nL55: cite17†Image L56: \nL57: Ali Abdaal Productivity Expert\nL58: ## Add friction to change your habits long-term\nL59: ### Interruptions: break your muscle memory\nL60: \nL61: Our interruptions are developed by top psychologists around the world – to help you use apps intentionally.\nL62: \nL63: Take a deep breath\nL64: \nL65: cite18†Image L66: \nL67: Take a deep breath\nL68: \nL69: Mirror\nL70: \nL71: cite18†Image L72: \nL73: Mirror\nL74: \nL75: Conversational Reflection\nL76: \nL77: cite18†Image L78: \nL79: Conversational Reflection\nL80: \nL81: Rotate Phone\nL82: \nL83: cite18†Image L84: \nL85: Rotate Phone\nL86: \nL87: Type random text\nL88: \nL89: cite18†Image L90: \nL91: Type random text\nL92: \nL93: 4-7-8 breathing\nL94: \nL95: cite18†Image L96: \nL97: 4-7-8 breathing\nL98: \nL99: Structured integration\nL100: \nL101: cite18†Image L102: \nL103: Structured integration\nL104: Lengo integration\nL105: \nL106: cite18†Image L107: \nL108: Lengo integration\nL109: ## Gain back 2 hours per day.\nL110: \nL111: You just wanted to check that one message – and ended up scrolling on Instagram for 30 minutes?\nL112: \nL113: Social media is designed to keep you hooked.\nL114: \nL115: Time that could be spent so much better – with your family and friends, getting work done or for your hobbies and wellbeing.\nL116: \nL117: cite7†Download one sec for free  → L118: \nL119: ## With powerful tools, you are in control of your attention\nL120: ### Block apps to avoid distractions\nL121: \nL122: When you need absolute focus, you can block distracting apps to your schedule.\nL123: \nL124: cite19†Image: Block apps completely with blocking mode L125: \nL126: ### Doomscroll emergency brake\nL127: \nL128: Set a time limit for your most addictive apps. one sec helps you to not get lost.\nL129: \nL130: cite20†Image: Block apps completely with blocking mode L131: \nL132: ## Establish healthy habits, break bad ones\nL133: \nL134: ### Healthy Alternatives\nL135: \nL136: Replace bad habits with good ones: one sec can nudge you towards healthy behavior.\nL137: ### Journaling\nL138: \nL139: Write down your thoughts and feelings to reflect and stay grounded.\nL140: \nL141: cite21†Image: Intention tracking L142: \nL143: ### Intention Tracking\nL144: \nL145: Set an intention for opening apps. By thinking ahead, you spend less time fooling around.\nL146: \nL147: cite22†Image: Emotion tracking L148: \nL149: ### Emotion Tracking\nL150: \nL151: Check in with yourself to reflect how your app usage affects your state of mind.\nL152: ## Stop getting distracted from work with our browser extension\nL153: \nL154: Distractions are everywhere. And all too often, disrupting your habits on your phone, makes you look for alternatives on the big screen.\nL155: \nL156: Don't worry, we got you covered. Our browser extension keeps your attention where you really want it.\nL157: \nL158: cite23†Check it out  → L159: \nL160: cite24†Image: browserExtension.imageAlt L161: \nL162: ## Integrate one sec into your workflow\nL163: ### cite25†Image: Structured app icon Structured\nL164: \nL165: With Structured connected, one sec will block you from opening distracting apps during uncompleted tasks.\nL166: \nL167: cite26†Image: Structured preview L168: \nL169: cite27†Learn more → L170: ### cite28†Image: Lengo app icon LENGO\nL171: \nL172: Our integration with Lengo helps you transform your toxic phone habits into productive language learning sessions.\nL173: \nL174: cite29†Image: Lengo preview L175: \nL176: cite30†Learn more  → L177: \nL178: > Everything You Need to Break Up With Your Phone\nL179: \nL180: cite31†↗†www.nytimes.com L181: \nL182: > I tried a new app that promised to cure my addiction to social media, and it actually worked\nL183: \nL184: cite32†↗†www.businessinsider.com L185: \nL186: Also endorsed by\nL187: \nL188: cite33†Image: Illustration indicating our commitment to research-driven development L189: ## Backed by university research\nL190: \nL191: Proven to reduce app usage by 57%\nL192: \nL193: As we are striving to make one sec as effective as possible, we have a dedicated in-house scientist doing research with universities across the globe. Their findings are unanimous: one sec works.\nL194: \nL195: cite2†View our research  → L196: ## Why one sec?\nL197: \nL198:   * ### Tackle the problem.\nL199: \nL200: Not just symptoms. Our app is designed to change your habits – and free you from social media distractions in the long term.\nL201: \nL202:   * ### Your data is private.\nL203: \nL204: Unlike other apps, we don‘t sell or analyze your data. Your usage data is yours, either offline or in a private cloud.\nL205: \nL206:   * ### Essentials are free.\nL207: \nL208: Convince yourself of one sec’s effect before spending a dime. It is not our goal to get rich over your phone addiction.\nL209: cite34†Image: one sec reduces your screen time long term L210: ## Still not convinced?\nL211: \nL212: With over 100K 5-star reviews, we're confident that one sec helps.\nL213: \nL214: This app works so well it is almost annoying. It blocks social media and if I need an extra boost, I use the strict block option. An amazing app.\nL215: \nL216: cite35†Image†ui-avatars.com L217: \nL218: Natalie App Store\nL219: \nL220: I just graduated as a doctor and was burning out. Since getting rid of social media with this app, my focus is coming back. Maybe gave me my life back.\nL221: \nL222: cite36†Image†ui-avatars.com L223: \nL224: Harold Singh App Store\nL225: Strict blockers never worked: I just turned them off. The forced pause is critical to be able to consciously make the choice though.\nL226: \nL227: cite37†Image†ui-avatars.com L228: \nL229: Jennnn...App Store\nL230: \nL231: I’ve struggled with serious addiction problems for years. After just one week, this app helped me with my unconscious scrolling. Forever grateful.\nL232: \nL233: cite38†Image†ui-avatars.com L234: \nL235: Kukopuk App Store\nL236: I have a dreadful phone addiction. Thanks to one sec, I reclaimed hours of my life for productivity and FUN. It made me happier and more productive.\nL237: \nL238: cite39†Image†ui-avatars.com L239: \nL240: Marsha App Store\nL241: \nL242: Using this for 2 years. The developer really cares and keeps finding creative solutions to set you free from the addictive nature of your phone.\nL243: \nL244: cite40†Image†ui-avatars.com L245: \nL246: Signifer34 App Store\nL247: I'm 16 and phone addiction is normalized. I decided I didn't want to waste my life away anymore. This app helped me out significantly.\nL248: \nL249: cite41†Image†ui-avatars.com L250: \nL251: Oscar App Store\nL252: \nL253: Premium is worth it considering how much 'time' I get. My average Instagram time dropped to just 8 mins a day. No more useless wasting of time.\nL254: \nL255: cite42†Image†ui-avatars.com L256: \nL257: Natanael App Store\nL258: I deleted other apps cold turkey, but YouTube felt different: I get value from it. one sec changed the dynamic. My screentime reduced by 45% in one week.\nL259: \nL260: cite43†Image†ui-avatars.com L261: \nL262: Artfvl App Store\nL263: \nL264: My screen time was over 19 hours a day. one sec cut it down by 17 HOURS. It breaks the muscle memory of unconsciously opening apps. It works.\nL265: \nL266: cite44†Image†ui-avatars.com L267: \nL268: Diva App Store\nL269: Strict blocking doesn't work—social media is embedded in life. One Sec forces a pause to think. It makes me intentional rather than impulsive.\nL270: \nL271: cite45†Image†ui-avatars.com L272: \nL273: Avaoir App Store\nL274: ## Frequently asked questions\nL275: \nL276: Can I also interrupt websites?\nL277: \nL278: Yes! With one sec's browser extension, you can set interventions for any website you like. By the way: There's an adult content filter to interrupt opening all kinds of adult websites.\nL279: \nL280: Can I customize one sec's interruptions?\nL281: Yes, you have loads of possibilities to adapt one sec to your personal needs! - Schedule interruption timings: when and for how long should the intervention appear? - Choose from a variety of interruption types: what happens when an app or website is intervened?\nL282: \nL283: Is one sec safe?\nL284: Absolutely. one sec focuses on building a high-quality product, not tracking or monetizing your data. All intervention logic runs locally on your device—there's no profiling, no data selling, and no investor pressure to push ads or analytics into the app.\nL285: \nL286: Can I block an app or website completely?\nL287: \nL288: Yes! You can set up blocks so that configured apps or sites can't be opened at all during a block session or scheduled time—giving you full focus with zero distractions.\nL289: \nL290: Can I use sec across several devices?\nL291: Yes, one sec works cross-device. You can set up the app for iOS and Android devices, and use the browser extension on Windows, macOS and Linux computers. With the same account, you can log in to different smartphones, tablets and computers and use them simultaneously. Pay once, use on all your devices.\nL292: \nL293: Can I buy one sec for my team / school?\nL294: Yes, you can! We offer team licenses for as low as 83ct per user per month (billed annually). Proceed to the cite46†checkout on our website†web.one-sec.app , select \"Multi-Seat Plan\" and add as many licenses as you need.\nL295: \nL296: cite47†View all questions  → L297: ## The science behind one sec:\nL298:   1. Grüning D. J., Riedel F., & Lorenz-Spreen P. (2023). Directing smartphone use through the s